<CaseStudy
  title="Bijgaardehof"
  subtitle="Three communities. Room for more than one way of living."
  heroPlaceholder="Bijgaardehof hero — courtyard in the factory shell (photo needed)"
  facts={facts}
  gallery={gallery}
  prev={{ href: '/brutopia/', label: 'Brutopia' }}
  next={{ href: '/village-homes/', label: 'Village Homes' }}
>
  <section class="case-reading mx-auto max-w-[820px] px-6 pt-12 sm:px-12">
    <p>Bijgaardehof opened in Ghent in 2022 with 59 homes across three cohousing groups—Biotope, De Spore and Wijgaard—and a neighborhood health center. BOGDAN’s project account describes collective indoor and outdoor spaces and a retained industrial setting beside the Bijgaardepark. <a class="source-link" href="https://www.bogdan.design/news/cohousingproject-bijgaardehof-in-use/">[Project source]</a></p>
    <p>It is a useful example of several communities sharing a development while retaining smaller group identities. A common address need not mean one giant household meeting. The health center adds a neighborhood-facing use with its own purpose and operating needs.</p>
    <h2>Share the garden. Keep the small meeting.</h2>
    <p>Different scales of sharing call for different decisions. A kitchen rota might belong to one group; roof repairs or a common path need agreement across the whole site. Draw that governance map alongside the site plan so nobody discovers their responsibilities only when the invoice arrives.</p>
    <p>A clinic is not simply a lively ground floor with a medical sign. It needs a viable provider, patient access, privacy, service connections and a durable lease. Identify those partners before putting the room into a Berkeley brief.</p>
    <aside class="research-note mt-8">Reading lens: project publications document the place and its intended design. They are not independent evaluations of affordability, energy performance or how every resident experiences life there. The questions here are our interpretation.</aside>
  </section>

  <Fragment slot="lessons">
<li>Separate household, group and whole-site decisions—and identify who pays at each level.</li>
<li>Bring a health or community service operator into feasibility, with its own access and privacy requirements.</li>
<li>Reuse can retain local character; verify what can actually be kept through surveys and engineering.</li>
</Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="sources"><a href="https://www.bogdan.design/news/cohousingproject-bijgaardehof-in-use/">BOGDAN · completed project announcement (2022)</a>. Reviewed October 2026. Approximate counts are identified; population changes over time.</Fragment>
</CaseStudy>
